editorialMd:
Build the result list

Sort readings by distance from 0, from nearest to farthest. This distance is called absolute value: both -4 and 4 have distance 4. When two numbers have the same distance, put the smaller number first. Keep all repeated numbers. For [3, -2, 2, -3], the result list is [-2, 2, -3, 3].

Return the answer

Add only numbers greater than 0 from the result list. Do not add 0 or negative numbers. Return the total, or 0 if no numbers match.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function magnitudeOrderPositiveInkInvoice(readings, k) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  out=[...a].sort((x,y)=>Math.abs(x)-Math.abs(y)||x-y);
  return out.reduce((s,x)=>s+Math.max(0,x),0);
}


